    "difficultyReason": "Parts a-c are direct one-step discount-multiplier calculations (0.9, 0.8, 0.7 of $240), with the tidy numbers and no choice of method. Part d is the hardest essential demand. It asks the student to compare each price with Jake's $190 and read 'smallest discount that works' as the first price at or below $190. The scaffolding does most of the work, because the three candidate discounts are already calculated in a-c, so no strategy has to be chosen. That makes it a little harder than the purely mechanical multipliers in p39-q13. It is clearly easier than p41-q21, where the student must set up and test the discount steps themselves.",
